│                       1. What the problem is                       │
│                                                                    │
│ The issue is that dmi_jtag_tap.sv introduces a generated trst_no   │
│ reset signal intended to allow reset via JTAG TestLogicReset state │
│ cycling, but the current implementation is unsafe.                 │
│                                                                    │
│ In particular:                                                     │
│                                                                    │
│  • trst_no is driven by logic that is not cleanly synchronized.    │
│  • It can glitch because it is updated from intermediate signals   │
│    in a way that depends on combinational and sequential           │
│    interactions.                                                   │
│  • That glitchy signal is then used as an asynchronous reset in    │
│    other modules (dmi_jtag.sv and dmi_cdc.sv), which is dangerous  │
│    and can cause incorrect reset behavior.                         │
│                                                                    │
│                    2. Where the problem occurs                     │
│                                                                    │
│ The problem is in src/dmi_jtag_tap.sv, around the                  │
│ trst_sync1/trst_sync2/trst_no logic:                               │
│                                                                    │
│  • trst_sync1 is assigned in always_comb                           │
│  • trst_sync2 and trst_no are updated in an always_ff @(posedge    │
│    tck_i or negedge trst_ni) block                                 │
│  • trst_no is driven directly from trst_sync2, which is not a      │
│    properly robust reset generation method here                    │
│  • trst_sync1 is combinationally forced high in TestLogicReset,    │
│    which can make the generated reset unstable/glitch-prone        │
│                                                                    │
│               3. Best practices to take into account               │
│                                                                    │
│ A safe fix should follow these principles:                         │
│                                                                    │
│  • Avoid generating asynchronous resets from combinational logic   │
│    unless absolutely necessary.                                    │
│  • If a reset must be derived from a clocked state machine, ensure │
│    it is registered cleanly and only updated on a clock edge.      │
│  • Do not mix combinational and sequential drivers for the same    │
│    reset-generation path.                                          │
│  • Ensure reset signals crossing into other domains are stable and │
│    glitch-free.                                                    │
│  • Prefer a simple, fully synchronous internal reset generation    │
│    scheme when the reset is intended to be derived from TAP state  │
│    transitions.                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│                     4. How to fix the problem                      │
│                                                                    │
│ The safest minimal fix is to replace the current                   │
│ trst_sync1/trst_sync2/trst_no generation with a properly           │
│ registered reset signal derived directly from the TAP state.       │
│                                                                    │
│ A good approach is:                                                │
│                                                                    │
│  • Remove the combinational dependency of trst_sync1 and the       │
│    two-stage partial reset logic.                                  │
│  • Register trst_no synchronously on tck_i.                        │
│  • Drive trst_no high when the TAP is in TestLogicReset, and low   │
│    otherwise.                                                      │
│  • Keep the external trst_ni as an asynchronous clear so the       │
│    design still resets correctly when the physical reset is        │
│    asserted.                                                       │
│                                                                    │
│ This preserves the intended functionality while removing the       │
│ glitch-prone path.                                                 │
