│                                                                    │
│                       Phase 1: FIX ANALYSIS                        │
│                                                                    │
│                       1) What the problem is                       │
│                                                                    │
│ The bug is in the new TAP reset logic introduced by commit         │
│ b5d1610. The design tries to generate trst_no from the JTAG TAP    │
│ state machine so that cycling TMS into TestLogicReset can reset    │
│ dmi_jtag. However, the implementation is unsafe because trst_no is │
│ driven inside logic that is not properly synchronized and is       │
│ updated in a way that can glitch.                                  │
│                                                                    │
│ That matters because trst_no is used as an asynchronous reset in   │
│ other modules (dmi_jtag.sv and dmi_cdc.sv). A glitch on an async   │
│ reset can cause spurious resets, metastability, or inconsistent    │
│ reset release across clock domains.                                │
│                                                                    │
│                    2) Where the problem occurs                     │
│                                                                    │
│ The problematic code is in src/dmi_jtag_tap.sv, around the new     │
│ trst_sync1/trst_sync2/trst_no logic:                               │
│                                                                    │
│  • trst_sync1 is assigned in combinational logic (always_comb)     │
│    based on TAP state.                                             │
│  • trst_sync2 and trst_no are updated in a clocked block.          │
│  • The current implementation mixes combinational and sequential   │
│    control in a way that can produce unstable/reset-like behavior. │
│  • In particular, trst_no is driven from a two-stage structure     │
│    that is not a clean synchronous reset generation path.          │
│                                                                    │
│               3) Best practices to take into account               │
│                                                                    │
│  • Do not generate asynchronous reset signals from combinational   │
│    logic that depends on state-machine outputs unless they are     │
│    fully synchronized and intentionally filtered.                  │
│  • If a reset must be derived from TAP state, generate it          │
│    synchronously and keep its assertion/deassertion clean and      │
│    clocked.                                                        │
│  • Avoid using partially initialized or combinationally-fed        │
│    intermediate signals to drive reset nets.                       │
│  • Prefer a simple registered signal that is asserted when the TAP │
│    is in TestLogicReset, then deasserted synchronously after the   │
│    state exits reset.                                              │
│  • Keep the fix minimal and localized.                             │
│                                                                    │
│                     4) How to fix the problem                      │
│                                                                    │
│ The safest fix is to remove the glitch-prone intermediate          │
│ reset-generation path and replace it with a straightforward        │
│ synchronous registered reset signal derived from the TAP state:    │
│                                                                    │
│  • declare trst_no as a registered signal if not already done      │
│    elsewhere,                                                      │
│  • assign it only in the TAP clocked process,                      │
│  • make it asserted while in TestLogicReset,                       │
│  • deassert it synchronously once the TAP exits that state.        │
│                                                                    │
│ This avoids combinational feedback and eliminates the glitch       │
│ hazard on the async reset path.                                    │
│                                                                    │
